<subsection xml:id="subsec-triple-integrals">
  <title>Triple Integrals</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The same Riemann-sum construction that defines double integrals over planar
      regions extends naturally to functions of three variables integrated over
      three-dimensional domains.
      A triple integral
      <me>
        \iiint\limits_V f(x,y,z)\,dV
      </me>
      sums the values of <m>f</m> over an infinite number of infinitesimally
      small volume elements <m>dV</m>.
      <!-- ADDED: sharpen the interpretation of general triple integrals -->
      When <m>f = 1</m>, the triple integral gives the ordinary volume of the
      domain. For a general integrand, it accumulates a density over the body,
      such as mass density or moment-of-inertia density. This is why triple
      integrals appear throughout physics as natural tools for computing mass,
      center of gravity, and moments of inertia of three-dimensional bodies.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-triple-integral-construction">
    <title>Construction of the Triple Integral</title>
    <p>
      Let <m>f\colon V \to \R</m> be a continuous function on a bounded spatial
      domain <m>V \subset \R^3</m>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Subdivide <m>V</m> into <m>n</m> small sub-domains of volumes
          <m>\Delta V_1, \ldots, \Delta V_n</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          In each sub-domain <m>V_k</m>, choose an arbitrary representative
          point <m>(x_k, y_k, z_k)</m> and form the approximation
          <m>f(x_k,y_k,z_k)\cdot\Delta V_k</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Sum over all sub-domains:
          <me>
            \iiint\limits_V f(x,y,z)\,dV
            \approx \sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta V_k.
          </me>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Passing to the limit as <m>n \to \infty</m> and each
      <m>\Delta V_k \to 0</m> gives the exact triple integral.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-triple-integral">
    <title>Triple Integral</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>V \subset \R^3</m> be a bounded domain and
        <m>f\colon V \to \R</m> a continuous function.
        The <term>triple integral</term> of <m>f</m> over <m>V</m> is:
        <me>
          \iiint\limits_V f(x,y,z)\,dV
          \;=\;
          \lim_{\substack{n \to \infty \\ \Delta V_k \to 0}}
          \sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta V_k.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Applications ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-volume-triple-integral-intuition">
    <title>Volume as Accumulated Unit Density</title>
    <p>
      A triple integral can be read as adding up contributions from tiny volume
      elements. If the integrand is the constant function <m>1</m>, each element
      contributes exactly its own volume <m>dV</m>. Summing all these pieces
      recovers the volume of the whole spatial domain.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-volume-triple-integral">
      <caption>
        The volume formula <m>\operatorname{vol}(V)=\iiint_V 1\,dV</m> adds
        the small volume elements <m>dV</m> filling the body <m>V</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-volume-triple-integral" width="55%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0,
            x={({cos(210)*0.7cm},{sin(210)*0.7cm})},
            y={({cos(330)*0.7cm},{sin(330)*0.7cm})},
            z={(0,0.85cm)}, font=\small]
            \tikzset{labelbox/.style={fill=white, fill opacity=0.9,
              text opacity=1, inner sep=1.2pt}}
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (3.0,0,0) node[below right] {$x$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,2.6,0) node[right] {$y$};
            \draw[->,thick] (0,0,0) -- (0,0,2.6) node[above] {$z$};
            \fill[blue!12, opacity=0.75]
              (0.4,0.4,0) -- (2.4,0.4,0) -- (2.4,1.8,0) -- (0.4,1.8,0) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!18, opacity=0.7]
              (0.4,0.4,1.7) -- (2.4,0.4,1.7) -- (2.4,1.8,1.7) -- (0.4,1.8,1.7) -- cycle;
            \foreach \x/\y in {0.4/0.4,2.4/0.4,2.4/1.8,0.4/1.8}
              \draw[blue!55!black] (\x,\y,0) -- (\x,\y,1.7);
            \draw[blue!55!black]
              (0.4,0.4,0) -- (2.4,0.4,0) -- (2.4,1.8,0) -- (0.4,1.8,0) -- cycle
              (0.4,0.4,1.7) -- (2.4,0.4,1.7) -- (2.4,1.8,1.7) -- (0.4,1.8,1.7) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!35, opacity=0.75]
              (1.0,0.85,0.55) -- (1.35,0.85,0.55) -- (1.35,1.15,0.55) -- (1.0,1.15,0.55) -- cycle;
            \fill[blue!45, opacity=0.75]
              (1.0,0.85,0.9) -- (1.35,0.85,0.9) -- (1.35,1.15,0.9) -- (1.0,1.15,0.9) -- cycle;
            \foreach \x/\y in {1.0/0.85,1.35/0.85,1.35/1.15,1.0/1.15}
              \draw[blue!70!black] (\x,\y,0.55) -- (\x,\y,0.9);
            \draw[blue!70!black]
              (1.0,0.85,0.55) -- (1.35,0.85,0.55) -- (1.35,1.15,0.55) -- (1.0,1.15,0.55) -- cycle
              (1.0,0.85,0.9) -- (1.35,0.85,0.9) -- (1.35,1.15,0.9) -- (1.0,1.15,0.9) -- cycle;
            \draw[blue!70!black, thin] (1.35,1.15,0.9) -- (2.15,1.45,1.25);
            \node[labelbox, blue!70!black, anchor=west] at (2.15,1.45,1.25) {$dV$};
            \draw[blue!55!black, thin] (2.4,1.8,1.7) -- (2.95,2.15,1.95);
            \node[labelbox, anchor=west] at (2.95,2.15,1.95) {$V$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-volume-triple-integral">
    <title>Volume of a Spatial Domain</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>volume</term> of a domain <m>V \subseteq \R^3</m> is:
        <me>
          \operatorname{vol}(V) = \iiint\limits_V 1\,dV.
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-geometric-center-3d-intuition">
    <title>Centroid as Average Position</title>
    <p>
      For a body of uniform density, the geometric center is the average position
      of all volume elements. The coordinate integrals add the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-,
      and <m>z</m>-coordinates of each small piece and then divide by the total
      volume.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-geometric-center-3d">
    <title>Geometric Center of a Spatial Domain</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The coordinates <m>(x_S, y_S, z_S)</m> of the
        <term>geometric center</term> (centroid) of <m>V \subseteq \R^3</m> are:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            x_S \amp= \frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(V)}\iiint\limits_V x\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            y_S \amp= \frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(V)}\iiint\limits_V y\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            z_S \amp= \frac{1}{\operatorname{vol}(V)}\iiint\limits_V z\,dV.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-mass-center-gravity-intuition">
    <title>Mass and Center of Gravity for Variable Density</title>
    <p>
      If density varies from point to point, equal volume elements no longer
      contribute equally to the balance point. The mass integral weights each
      small volume element by its density <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m>. The center of
      gravity is then the density-weighted average position, so it is pulled
      toward regions where the body is heavier.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-mass-center-gravity">
    <title>Mass and Center of Gravity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a body <m>V \subseteq \R^3</m> with variable mass density
        <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> (mass per unit volume), the <term>total mass</term>
        and <term>center of gravity</term> are:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            m \amp= \iiint\limits_V \rho(x,y,z)\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            x_S \amp= \frac{1}{m}\iiint\limits_V x\cdot\rho(x,y,z)\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            y_S \amp= \frac{1}{m}\iiint\limits_V y\cdot\rho(x,y,z)\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            z_S \amp= \frac{1}{m}\iiint\limits_V z\cdot\rho(x,y,z)\,dV.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-center-gravity-vs-centroid">
    <p>
      If the density <m>\rho</m> is constant throughout the body, the center of
      gravity coincides with the geometric center. A non-uniform density shifts
      the center of gravity towards regions of higher density.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark xml:id="rem-moments-inertia-intuition">
    <title>Moments of Inertia as Weighted Squared Distance</title>
    <p>
      Rotating a body about an axis is harder when more mass lies far away from
      that axis. A moment of inertia therefore weights each small mass element
      by the square of its perpendicular distance to the axis. For example, the
      distance from <m>(x,y,z)</m> to the <m>z</m>-axis is
      <m>\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, so the corresponding squared distance is
      <m>x^2+y^2</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-moments-inertia">
    <title>Moments of Inertia</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For a body <m>V</m> with density <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m>, the moments of
        inertia with respect to the <m>x</m>-, <m>y</m>-, and <m>z</m>-axes are:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            J_x \amp= \iiint\limits_V (y^2+z^2)\,\rho(x,y,z)\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            J_y \amp= \iiint\limits_V (x^2+z^2)\,\rho(x,y,z)\,dV,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            J_z \amp= \iiint\limits_V (x^2+y^2)\,\rho(x,y,z)\,dV.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-triple-integrals-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-triple-integral-mass-meaning">
      <title>Meaning of a Triple Integral with Density</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> is a mass density on a body <m>V</m>, what does
          <me>
            \iiint_V \rho(x,y,z)\,dV
          </me>
          represent?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>The total mass of the body</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. Integrating density over volume gives mass.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The surface area of the body</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Surface area is not computed by a volume integral.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The center of gravity directly</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The center of gravity requires moment integrals divided by the mass.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>The moment of inertia about every axis at once</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Moments of inertia need extra distance factors.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

  </exercises>

</subsection>
